---
// REVIEW: Layer 2 interpretation for the Beighton score, drafted for Craig's
// review. Static, always visible, rendered at build time. FAQ answers come
// from beighton-score.content.ts and are shown word for word (they also feed
// the page's FAQPage JSON-LD).
import { CUTOFFS, FAQ, SOURCES } from '../../tools/beighton-score.content';
import '../../styles/interpretation.css';

const s = SOURCES;
---

<!-- REVIEW -->
<section class="interpretation" aria-labelledby="understanding-beighton">
	<h2 id="understanding-beighton">Understanding your Beighton score</h2>

	<h3 id="what-it-measures">What the score measures</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.measures.a}</p>
	<p>
		It is the most widely recognised tool for generalised joint hypermobility and the entry point to the 2017 criteria
		for hypermobile Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (<a href={s.malfait.href} rel="noopener">{s.malfait.label}</a>).
	</p>

	<h3 id="score-bands">Score bands</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.cutoff.a}</p>
	<table class="interp-table">
		<caption>Beighton cut-offs for generalised joint hypermobility (2017 hEDS criteria)</caption>
		<thead>
			<tr><th scope="col">Group</th><th scope="col">Cut-off</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			{CUTOFFS.map(c => <tr><td>{c.group}</td><td>{c.cutoff}</td></tr>)}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		The published groups are defined by puberty, not exact age, so this page's age options are an approximation. If you
		are one point short, the <a href="/tools/five-part-questionnaire/">five-part questionnaire</a> can make up the
		difference.
	</p>

	<h3 id="change">What a change in score means</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.change.a}</p>

	<h3 id="what-it-does-not-measure">What it does not measure</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.self.a} (<a href={s.juul.href} rel="noopener">{s.juul.label}</a>)</p>
	<p>
		It does not measure pain, instability, dislocations or joints it does not test, such as shoulders, hips or jaw. The
		<a href="/tools/heds-criteria/">hEDS criteria checklist</a> shows how the score fits with the other criteria.
	</p>

	<h3 id="taking-it-to-your-gp">Taking this to your GP or consultant</h3>
	<p>
		Bring the total, the date, your age group, which manoeuvres scored, and any history of joints that used to bend
		further, dislocations or sprains. Most GPs know the Beighton score, but ask for a self-score to be checked by
		examination. With symptoms, a score at or above the cut-off can support a referral to physiotherapy or
		rheumatology; on its own it diagnoses nothing. See also
		<a href="/nhs/preparing-for-gp-appointment/">preparing for a GP appointment</a>.
	</p>

	<h3 id="sources">Sources</h3>
	<ul class="interp-sources">
		<li>Beighton P, Solomon L, Soskolne CL. Articular mobility in an African population. <em>Ann Rheum Dis</em> 1973;32(5):413–418. <a href={s.beighton.href} rel="noopener">DOI</a></li>
		<li>Malfait F, Francomano C, Byers P, et al. The 2017 international classification of the Ehlers-Danlos syndromes. <em>Am J Med Genet C Semin Med Genet</em> 2017;175(1):8–26. <a href={s.malfait.href} rel="noopener">DOI</a></li>
		<li>Juul-Kristensen B, Schmedling K, Rombaut L, Lund H, Engelbert RH. Measurement properties of clinical assessment methods for classifying generalized joint hypermobility: a systematic review. <em>Am J Med Genet C Semin Med Genet</em> 2017;175(1):116–147. <a href={s.juul.href} rel="noopener">DOI</a></li>
	</ul>
</section>
